# --- Secrets (copy to ".env", never commit ".env") ---
GOOGLE_STITCH_API_KEY=

# --- How this ties to MCP (same idea as .env / .env.example) ---
#
# • .cursor/mcp.json.example — committed template: shape of MCP config, no secrets.
# • .cursor/mcp.json — your real config; in this repo it matches the example and
#   only references ${env:GOOGLE_STITCH_API_KEY} (safe to commit).
# • Put the actual key in .env as GOOGLE_STITCH_API_KEY, then make sure Cursor’s
#   process can see that variable (shell profile, launch from terminal after
#   `source .env`, direnv, etc.). Remote MCP does not read .env by itself.
#
# If env injection is awkward on your machine, you can put the same JSON in
# ~/.cursor/mcp.json with a literal header value — that file stays local.
